ol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-tool\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M14.7 6.3a4 4 0 0 0-5.4 5.4L3 18v3h3l6.3-6.3a4 4 0 0 0 5.4-5.4l-2.5 2.5-2.5-2.5z\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-clip\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"4\" width=\"14\" height=\"17\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M9 4V3h6v1M9 10h6M9 14h6M9 18h3\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-check\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"m5 12.5 4.5 4.5L19 7.5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-minus\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M6 12h12\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-info\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"9\"/><path d=\"M12 11v6M12 7.5v.5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-star\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M12 2.5l2.9 6.1 6.6.8-4.9 4.6 1.3 6.6L12 17.3l-5.9 3.3 1.3-6.6-4.9-4.6 6.6-.8z\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-down\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M12 4v12M6 10l6 6 6-6M5 20h14\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-cert\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"4\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M7 8h10M7 11h6M15 16v5l2-1.5 2 1.5v-5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-family\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><circle cx=\"7\" cy=\"6\" r=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"17\" cy=\"6\" r=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M3 20c0-4 2-7 4-7s4 3 4 7M13 20c0-4 2-7 4-7s4 3 4 7\"/></symbol>\n</svg>\n\n", "header": "<header class=\"site-head\">\n    <a class=\"logo\" href=\"/en\" aria-label=\"Ocean Way, home\"><svg viewBox=\"2 2 116 116\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><defs><clipPath id=\"logo-clip\"><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"60\" r=\"47.01\"/></clipPath></defs><g clip-path=\"url(#logo-clip)\" fill=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"72.3\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"54.6\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"11\"/><path d=\"M28 59 C36 36 72 23.5 118 32 L118 51 C84 41 47 43.5 28 59Z\"/></g><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"60\" r=\"52.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"11\"/></svg></a>\n    <nav class=\"site-nav\" aria-label=\"Main\">\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#courses\">Courses</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#passages\">Passages</a>\n      <a class=\"navl on\" href=\"/en/theory\">Theory</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#fleet\">Fleet</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"#\">Prices</a>\n    </nav>\n    <div class=\"site-tools\">\n      <details class=\"lang\" translate=\"no\"><summary class=\"round\" aria-label=\"Language: English\">EN</summary><ul class=\"lang-menu\"><li><a href=\"/en/theory/tides\" hreflang=\"en\" lang=\"en\" aria-current=\"true\">English<span>EN</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/ru/theory/tides\" hreflang=\"ru\" lang=\"ru\">Русский<span>RU</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/de/theory/tides\" hreflang=\"de\" lang=\"de\">Deutsch<span>DE</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/es/theory/tides\" hreflang=\"es\" lang=\"es\">Español<span>ES</span></a></li></ul></details>\n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" id=\"themeBtn\" aria-label=\"Theme\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-theme\"/></svg></button>\n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Saved\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-heart\"/></svg></button>\n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Account\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-user\"/></svg></button>\n    </div>\n  </header>", "main": "\n    <article>\n      <header class=\"art-head\">\n        <nav class=\"crumbs\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><ol><li><a href=\"/en\">Home</a></li><li><a href=\"/en/theory\">Theory</a></li><li aria-current=\"page\">Weather and navigation</li></ol></nav>\n        <h1 class=\"display\">Tides: heights, streams and the rule of twelfths</h1>\n        <p class=\"lead\">Even if you learn to sail where the tide barely moves, the Yachtmaster syllabus and northern waters expect you to work it out. Here is how tides rise and fall, how to read chart datum and tidal streams, and how to time a bar crossing.</p>\n        <div class=\"byline\"><span class=\"meta\" style=\"color:var(--ink)\">Ocean Way instructors</span><span class=\"meta\">Updated 1 Nov 2026</span><span class=\"meta\">6 min read</span><span class=\"lvl\">Coastal skipper</span></div>\n      </header>\n      <div class=\"art-grid\">\n        <div class=\"art-body\">\n          <details class=\"art-mobile-toc band\"><summary>On this page<svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-chev\"/></svg></summary><ol class=\"toc-list\"><li><a href=\"#why-tides\">Why there are tides</a></li><li><a href=\"#springs-neaps\">Springs and neaps</a></li><li><a href=\"#chart-datum\">Chart datum</a></li><li><a href=\"#tide-tables\">Tide tables</a></li><li><a href=\"#twelfths\">Rule of twelfths</a></li><li><a href=\"#clearance\">Crossing a bar</a></li><li><a href=\"#streams\">Tidal streams</a></li><li><a href=\"#mediterranean\">Our home waters</a></li><li><a href=\"#practise\">Practise it on the water</a></li></ol></details>\n          <div class=\"band summary\"><span class=\"kicker\">In short</span><ul><li>The moon and sun cause the tides; springs follow full and new moon.</li><li>Depth of water is charted depth plus height of tide.</li><li>The rule of twelfths: 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1 twelfths of the range each hour.</li><li>Wind against tide makes short, steep seas.</li></ul></div>\n          <section id=\"why-tides\">\n<h2>Why there are tides</h2>\n<p>Tides are the regular rise and fall of the sea, caused mainly by the pull of the moon and, to a lesser extent, the sun. The moon draws the oceans into a bulge on the side of the Earth facing it, and a second bulge forms on the far side. As the Earth turns, coasts pass through both.</p>\n<p>So on most Atlantic and North Sea coasts there are two high waters and two low waters in a little over 24 hours, about 50 minutes later each day. From low water to high water takes roughly six hours.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>High water (HW).</b> The highest level the tide reaches on one cycle.</li>\n<li><b>Low water (LW).</b> The lowest level on one cycle.</li>\n<li><b>Range.</b> The difference in height between a high water and the next low water. A big range means more water moving and stronger streams.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"springs-neaps\">\n<h2>Springs and neaps</h2>\n<p>At new moon and full moon, the sun and moon line up with the Earth and their pulls add together. You get spring tides: higher high waters, lower low waters, a bigger range and stronger streams. The name has nothing to do with the season.</p>\n<p>At half moon they pull at right angles and partly cancel, giving neap tides with a small range and weaker streams. The cycle from springs to neaps and back takes about two weeks, and the biggest springs usually come a day or two after full or new moon.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"chart-datum\">\n<h2>Chart datum and drying heights</h2>\n<p><b>Chart datum (CD).</b> The level from which charted depths are measured. On most modern charts it is Lowest Astronomical Tide (LAT), the lowest level the tide is predicted to reach in average weather. So there is usually more water than the chart shows, rarely less.</p>\n<p><b>Charted depth.</b> A depth printed in the sea, measured down from chart datum.</p>\n<p><b>Drying height.</b> Banks and mudflats that are uncovered at chart datum show an underlined figure: their height above chart datum.</p>\n<p><b>Height of tide.</b> How far the sea surface is above chart datum at a given moment. It ties everything together:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Depth of water = charted depth + height of tide.</li>\n<li>Depth over a drying area = height of tide − drying height.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Heights of land, lights and bridge clearances are measured from a high-water level instead. The chart notes say which.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"tide-tables\">\n<h2>Tide tables and ports</h2>\n<p>Tide tables in almanacs and apps give the daily times and heights of high and low water. They are predictions, and weather can move the real level away from them.</p>\n<p><b>Standard ports.</b> The main harbours, with full daily predictions and a tidal curve (a graph of height between low and high water).</p>\n<p><b>Secondary ports.</b> Smaller harbours, worked out by applying the almanac's time and height differences to a nearby standard port.</p>\n<p>Check the time zone. Many tables use UT (Universal Time), so add the difference for local or summer time.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"twelfths\">\n<h2>The rule of twelfths</h2>\n<p>The tide rises slowly at first, fastest in the middle, then slowly again. The rule of twelfths estimates the height each hour, assuming about six hours from low to high water. Divide the range into twelve parts. Hour by hour, the tide rises by 1, 2, 3, 3, 2 and 1 twelfths, and falls in the same pattern after high water.</p>\n<p>Worked example: low water is at 0800, height 1.0 metre. High water is at 1400, height 5.8 metres. The range is 4.8 metres, so one twelfth is 0.4 metres.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Rise in this hour</th><th>Height of tide</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>0800 (low water)</td><td>None</td><td>1.0 m</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0900</td><td>1/12 = 0.4 m</td><td>1.4 m</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1000</td><td>2/12 = 0.8 m</td><td>2.2 m</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1100</td><td>3/12 = 1.2 m</td><td>3.4 m</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1200</td><td>3/12 = 1.2 m</td><td>4.6 m</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1300</td><td>2/12 = 0.8 m</td><td>5.4 m</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1400 (high water)</td><td>1/12 = 0.4 m</td><td>5.8 m</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Half the range comes in the two middle hours. The rule is only an approximation. Where the tidal curve is irregular, as in parts of the Solent, use the curve from the almanac instead.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"clearance\">\n<h2>Clearance and crossing a bar</h2>\n<p>Many harbours have a bar (a bank of sand or shingle across the entrance) you can only cross with enough tide.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the charted depth over the bar. Say it is 0.7 metres.</li>\n<li>Take your draught (the depth of the keel below the waterline). Say 1.9 metres.</li>\n<li>Add an under-keel clearance (safety margin). Use at least a metre over a bar, more in swell, because the boat drops into each trough.</li>\n<li>So you need 1.9 + 1.0 = 2.9 metres of water. The bar already has 0.7 metres at chart datum, so the height of tide must be at least 2.9 − 0.7 = 2.2 metres.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>From the table, the height of tide reaches 2.2 metres at 1000. If the falling tide mirrors the rise, it drops back to 2.2 metres at about 1800. So the window is roughly 1000 to 1800. Aim for the middle of it, not the edges.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"streams\">\n<h2>Tidal streams</h2>\n<p>As the tide rises and falls, water flows along the coast and in and out of estuaries. This is the tidal stream. The flood runs while the tide is rising, the ebb while it is falling, and the pause between them is slack water. A stream is described by its set (the direction it flows towards, the opposite of how wind is named) and its rate in knots.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Tidal diamonds.</b> A letter in a diamond on the chart. A table on the chart gives the set and rate there for each hour before and after high water at a standard port, at springs and at neaps.</li>\n<li><b>Tidal stream atlases.</b> A booklet with a chartlet for each hour of the tide. Arrows show direction, and figures such as 08,15 give the rate in tenths of a knot: 0.8 knots at neaps, 1.5 at springs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Spring rates are often around twice neap rates. Streams are strongest off headlands and in narrow channels, and do not always turn at high or low water.</p>\n<h3>Wind against tide</h3>\n<p>When the wind blows against the stream, the waves become short, steep and close together. A Force 5 that is pleasant with the tide can be rough against a spring ebb, and off headlands you may meet overfalls (breaking water where the stream runs over an uneven bottom). Round headlands at slack water or with wind and stream together.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"mediterranean\">\n<h2>Tides in our home waters</h2>\n<p>In most of the Mediterranean and Aegean, including the Turkish coast and the Greek islands, the tidal range is small, typically under half a metre. It is larger in a few places: close to a metre in the northern Adriatic and up to around two metres in the Gulf of Gabès in Tunisia.</p>\n<p>So here, other things usually matter more. Strong wind can pile water against a coast or push it away. Low pressure raises the sea and high pressure lowers it, by roughly a centimetre per hectopascal. Currents run through straits, and a long meltemi sets up a surface drift.</p>\n<p>But tides are a core part of the Yachtmaster Coastal syllabus, and you need them the moment you sail in the Atlantic, the English Channel or northern Europe.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"practise\">\n<h2>Practise it on the water</h2>\n<p>On our Yachtmaster Coastal courses you work through tidal height and stream problems with almanac extracts and practice charts. On the water you compare the depth sounder with the chart and plan each passage as if the tide mattered, so you are ready for tidal waters.</p>\n</section>\n\n          <div class=\"art-mobile-course\"><div class=\"band art-course\"><span class=\"kicker\">Practise it on</span><div class=\"card-title\">Yachtmaster Coastal</div><p>Learn this on the water with an instructor, in a week living aboard.</p><a class=\"pill pill-primary pill-wide pill-sm\" href=\"/en#courses\">See the courses</a></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <aside class=\"art-side\" aria-label=\"On this page\"><span class=\"kicker\">On this page</span><ol class=\"toc-list\"><li><a href=\"#why-tides\">Why there are tides</a></li><li><a href=\"#springs-neaps\">Springs and neaps</a></li><li><a href=\"#chart-datum\">Chart datum</a></li><li><a href=\"#tide-tables\">Tide tables</a></li><li><a href=\"#twelfths\">Rule of twelfths</a></li><li><a href=\"#clearance\">Crossing a bar</a></li><li><a href=\"#streams\">Tidal streams</a></li><li><a href=\"#mediterranean\">Our home waters</a></li><li><a href=\"#practise\">Practise it on the water</a></li></ol><div class=\"band art-course\"><span class=\"kicker\">Practise it on</span><div class=\"card-title\">Yachtmaster Coastal</div><p>Learn this on the water with an instructor, in a week living aboard.</p><a class=\"pill pill-primary pill-wide pill-sm\" href=\"/en#courses\">See the courses</a></div></aside>\n      </div>\n    </article>\n    <section class=\"sec\" style=\"margin-top:96px\">\n      <div class=\"sec-head row-head\"><div><span class=\"kicker\">Theory</span><h2 class=\"h2\" style=\"margin-top:12px\">Keep reading</h2></div><div class=\"row-nav\"><a class=\"pill pill-quiet\" href=\"/en/theory\">All theory</a></div></div>\n      <div class=\"hscroll\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"More theory\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/weather\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1500375592092-40eb2168fd21?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A wave breaking in clear water\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Bareboat skipper</span><span class=\"card-title\">Reading the weather: Beaufort, forecasts, meltemi</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Learn the Beaufort scale, where to get Mediterranean sailing forecasts, how to read gusts, and how the meltemi, sirocco and bora affect a 40 ft yacht.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/position-fixing\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1534008897995-27a23e859048?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"Boats in a bay between cliffs\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Coastal skipper</span><span class=\"card-title\">Where are we? Position fixing and dead reckoning</span><span class=\"card-meta\">How to fix a yacht's position on a paper chart: latitude and longitude, dead reckoning, estimated position, bearing fixes, transits and compass error.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/passage-planning\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1764726411645-744d48aa4497?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A sextant, watch and dividers on a chart\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Coastal skipper</span><span class=\"card-title\">Planning a passage</span><span class=\"card-meta\">How to plan a yacht passage: charts and pilot books, route and waypoints, clearing bearings, timing for daylight and the meltemi, bolt holes and the hourly log.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/points-of-sail\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1540946485063-a40da27545f8?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A sailing yacht close-hauled on open water\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span><span class=\"card-title\">Points of sail: how a yacht sails close to the wind</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Learn the points of sail from close-hauled to running: the no-go zone, sail trim on each point, tacking and gybing, apparent wind and reading telltales.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/knots\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1563296291-14f26f10c20f?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A schooner with all sails set\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span><span class=\"card-title\">Six knots every crew member uses</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Six sailing knots every crew member needs: bowline, clove hitch, round turn and two half hitches, cleat hitch, figure-of-eight and reef knot, step by step.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/tacking-gybing\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1777880652269-ff1dd5e65fbb?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"Two sailors at the wheel under sail\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span><span class=\"card-title\">Tacking and gybing, step by step</span><span class=\"card-meta\">How to tack and gybe a sailing yacht: the calls, crew jobs, winches without riding turns, a controlled gybe, getting out of irons and tips for catamarans.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a></div>\n    </section>\n  ", "footer": "<footer class=\"site-foot\">\n    <div class=\"foot-row\">\n      <span class=\"meta\">Ocean Way · Layout prototype. Prices, dates and people are sample content. Photos via Unsplash.</span>\n      <nav class=\"foot-links\"><a href=\"#\">About</a><a href=\"#\">Telegram</a><a href=\"#\">Privacy</a></nav>\n    </div>\n  </footer>", "scripts": ["\nconst root = document.documentElement;\ndocument.getElementById('themeBtn').addEventListener('click', () => { if (root.dataset.theme === 'night') delete root.dataset.theme; else root.dataset.theme = 'night'; });\nif (new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('theme') === 'night') root.dataset.theme = 'night';\n(() => {\n  const links = [...document.querySelectorAll('.art-side .toc-list a')]; if (!links.length) return;\n  const secs = links.map((a) => document.getElementById(a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)));\n  const io = new IntersectionObserver((es) => es.forEach((e) => { if (e.isIntersecting) links.forEach((a, i) => a.classList.toggle('on', secs[i] === e.target)); }), { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -70% 0px' });\n  secs.forEach((s) => s && io.observe(s));\n})();\n"], "tail": "", "styles": "theory"}